# Cuentos de Estudios Sociales de 6.º (9 a 12)
from personajes6 import *

CUENTOS = [
dict(area="sociales", n=9, slug="tenochtitlan", std="SS.6-8.G.4 · SS.6-8.H.4.LC",
  std_texto="explain how the Mexica changed their environment to build Tenochtitlan, and tell the difference between things that happen together and things that cause each other.",
  titulo="A city on a lake", titulo_es="Una ciudad sobre un lago",
  entrada="A story about Tenochtitlan, the Mexica city built on a lake, and why Mexico City is sinking today.",
  entrada_es="Un cuento sobre Tenochtitlan, la ciudad mexica construida sobre un lago, y por qué la Ciudad de México se hunde hoy.",
  tema="The Mexica adapted to a lake, and changes still have effects today", tema_es="Los mexicas se adaptaron a un lago, y los cambios aún tienen efectos hoy",
  descripcion="A Grade 6 social studies story: students learn how the Mexica built Tenochtitlan on a lake, and why Mexico City is sinking today, telling causes apart from things that just happen together. With key words, glossary, questions, an art connection and optional help in Spanish.",
  alt="In the classroom, Israel and Selena build a model city of clay on a tray of water with tiny green gardens, while Ms. Haddad shows a painting of an eagle on a cactus.",
  escena=f"A sixth grade classroom in Chicago: on a big tray of blue water, a model of an island city made of clay, with tiny temples, straight canals, little green rectangle gardens and long narrow roads crossing the water to the shore; {ISRAEL}, carefully places a tiny green garden in the water; {SELENA}, adds a small clay canoe; {P_HADDAD}, holds up a colorful picture of an eagle standing on a cactus that grows from a rock in water",
  palabras=[
    ("adapt","adapt","To change the way you live so you can survive in a place.","adaptarse","Cambiar la forma de vivir para poder sobrevivir en un lugar."),
    ("chinampa","chinampa","A rectangle of farmland built in shallow lake water with layers of mud and plants.","chinampa","Un rectángulo de tierra de cultivo construido en el agua poco profunda de un lago, con capas de lodo y plantas."),
    ("causeway","causeway","A raised road built across water or wet land.","calzada","Un camino elevado construido sobre el agua o sobre tierra mojada."),
    ("aqueduct","aqueduct","A channel or pipe that carries fresh water from far away to a city.","acueducto","Un canal o tubo que lleva agua dulce desde lejos hasta una ciudad."),
    ("cause","cause","Something that makes another thing happen.","causa","Algo que hace que otra cosa pase."),
    ("correlation","correlation","When two things happen at the same time or change together, even if one does not cause the other.","correlación","Cuando dos cosas pasan al mismo tiempo o cambian juntas, aunque una no cause la otra.")],
  parrafos=[
    '''Ms. Haddad held up a picture of an eagle standing on a cactus. "Have you seen this before?" "It's on the flag of Mexico!" said Selena. "Right," said Ms. Haddad. {{a|"The Mexica, sometimes called the Aztecs, said that their god told them to build a city where they saw an eagle on a cactus. They said they saw it on a small, swampy island in Lake Texcoco, and around 1325, they began building Tenochtitlan there."}}''',
    '''Israel frowned. "Why build a city in a lake? Seems like a bad idea." "It had problems," said Ms. Haddad, "but also advantages. The water protected them from enemies, and the lake was full of fish, birds and plants." {{b|"The Mexica didn't move somewhere easier. They chose to [[adapt|adapt]] to the lake, and to change it."}}''',
    '''There wasn't enough land to grow food, so the Mexica made more. {{c|"They built [[chinampa|chinampas]]," said Ms. Haddad. "Farmers marked out rectangles in the shallow water, piled up layers of mud and plants, and planted trees along the edges so their roots would hold everything in place."}} The chinampas were so rich that farmers could grow corn, beans, squash and flowers several times a year. Some chinampas are still farmed in Xochimilco, in Mexico City, today.''',
    '''Israel and Selena built a clay model on a tray of water. "How did people get to the shore?" asked Selena. "On [[causeway|causeways]]," said Ms. Haddad, "long raised roads across the water, with bridges that could be lifted if enemies came." And because the lake water was salty in many places, the Mexica built an [[aqueduct|aqueduct]] that carried fresh water from springs at Chapultepec, a hill on the shore.''',
    '''By 1519, when Spanish soldiers arrived, Tenochtitlan had about 200,000 people, more than almost any city in Europe at the time. The Spanish conquered the city in 1521 and built Mexico City on top of it. Over the next few hundred years, people drained most of the lake to stop the floods.''',
    '''Then Ms. Haddad showed a photo of an old church in Mexico City, leaning to one side. "Today, parts of Mexico City sink up to 20 inches a year." Daniel, at the next table, said, "That's because the city got so big and heavy." {{d|Ms. Haddad smiled. "Good thinking, but careful. The city grew bigger, and the city sank more. Those two things happened together. That's a [[correlation|correlation]]. It doesn't prove one caused the other."}}''',
    '''"So what's the real [[cause|cause]]?" asked Selena. {{e|"The city is built on the soft clay of the old lake," said Ms. Haddad. "For many years, the city has pumped water out from under the ground to drink. As the water is pumped out, the clay dries and squeezes together, like a sponge, and the ground sinks."}} "So more people did matter," said Daniel, "because more people needed more water." "Yes! That's a cause with more than one step."''',
    '''Israel looked at their model city floating on the tray. "The Mexica adapted to the lake, and then the lake was taken away," he said. "And now the lake's old clay is still changing the city." Selena put one last tiny canoe in the water. "Seven hundred years later," she said, "the lake is still in charge."'''],
  preguntas=[
    ("a","Why did the Mexica build Tenochtitlan on an island?","¿Por qué los mexicas construyeron Tenochtitlan en una isla?",
     ["They said they saw an eagle on a cactus there","It was the only place with flat land and no water","The Spanish soldiers told them to go there"],0,
     "The Mexica said that their god told them to build a city where they saw an eagle on a cactus, and they saw it on an island in Lake Texcoco."),
    ("b","What did the Mexica do instead of moving somewhere easier?","¿Qué hicieron los mexicas en lugar de ir a un lugar más fácil?",
     ["They dried up the lake right away","They adapted to the lake and changed it","They asked other cities for food every day"],1,
     "The Mexica chose to adapt to the lake and to change it."),
    ("c","How did farmers build chinampas?","¿Cómo construían las chinampas los campesinos?",
     ["They dug holes in dry hills for seeds","They floated wooden boats full of soil","They piled mud and plants in shallow water"],2,
     "Farmers marked rectangles in the shallow water, piled up mud and plants, and planted trees along the edges."),
    ("d","Why did Ms. Haddad say Daniel's idea showed only a correlation?","¿Por qué la maestra dijo que la idea de Daniel solo mostraba una correlación?",
     ["The city did not really grow bigger","Two things together aren't proof of a cause","Heavy cities never sink into the ground"],1,
     "The city grew and the city sank at the same time, but two things happening together doesn't prove one caused the other."),
    ("e","What is the real cause of Mexico City sinking?","¿Cuál es la causa real de que la Ciudad de México se hunda?",
     ["The old canals are filling up with rain","The lake is slowly filling up again under the city","Pumping water dries and squeezes the clay"],2,
     "The city pumps water from under the ground, and the soft clay of the old lake dries and squeezes together, so the ground sinks.")],
  reto=dict(tipo="Cause or correlation?", tipo_es="¿Causa o correlación?",
    pregunta="Explain the difference between a cause and a correlation, using Mexico City as an example. Then make up your own example of two things that happen together without one causing the other.",
    pregunta_es="Explica la diferencia entre una causa y una correlación, usando como ejemplo la Ciudad de México. Después inventa tu propio ejemplo de dos cosas que pasan juntas sin que una cause la otra.",
    pista="A correlation: the city grew and the city sank at the same time. The cause: pumping water from under the ground dries the clay, so the ground sinks. Your own example could be: \"In summer, people eat more ice cream and more people go swimming.\" Ice cream does not cause swimming; hot weather causes both."),
  mdc=dict(pregunta="The Mexica changed the lake by building chinampas, and the Spanish changed it by draining it. Compare the two changes: what was the goal of each one, and what were the effects, short-term and long-term?",
    pregunta_es="Los mexicas cambiaron el lago construyendo chinampas, y los españoles lo cambiaron secándolo. Compara los dos cambios: ¿cuál era el objetivo de cada uno y cuáles fueron sus efectos a corto y a largo plazo?",
    pista="Chinampas: the goal was to make farmland; the short-term effect was lots of food, and the long-term effect is that some are still farmed today. Draining: the goal was to stop floods; the short-term effect was more dry land for the city, but the long-term effect is that the city sits on soft clay that sinks when water is pumped out. Both groups changed the environment, but one change worked with the lake and the other worked against it."),
  pausa=dict(emoji="🖼️", arte="Visual arts · VA:Cn11.1.6",
    intro="Look at the first picture page of the Codex Mendoza, a book painted by Indigenous artists in Mexico around 1540, about 20 years after the Spanish conquest. It shows the founding of Tenochtitlan.",
    intro_es="Observa la primera página con dibujos del Códice Mendoza, un libro pintado por artistas indígenas en México hacia 1540, unos 20 años después de la conquista española. Muestra la fundación de Tenochtitlan.",
    enlace=("https://worldhistorycommons.org/node/40","See the Codex Mendoza page (World History Commons)"),
    describe="In the center, an eagle stands on a cactus growing from a rock. Blue bands of water form an X that divides the square into four parts, like the four parts of the city. Men sit around the eagle on reed mats. Around the edges is a border of small boxes, each a sign for a year.",
    describe_es="En el centro, un águila está parada sobre un nopal que crece en una piedra. Unas franjas azules de agua forman una X que divide el cuadro en cuatro partes, como las cuatro partes de la ciudad. Alrededor del águila hay hombres sentados sobre petates. En las orillas hay un borde de cajitas, y cada una es el signo de un año.",
    preguntas=[("Why do you think the artists put the eagle in the very center of the page?","¿Por qué crees que los artistas pusieron el águila justo en el centro de la página?"),
               ("This page was painted after the Spanish conquest, partly for Spanish readers. How might that change what the artists chose to show?","Esta página se pintó después de la conquista española, en parte para lectores españoles. ¿Cómo podría eso cambiar lo que los artistas decidieron mostrar?")],
    pista="The eagle on the cactus is the sign of the founding, so the artists put it in the center to show it was the heart of the story and of the city. The water lines remind us that the city was built on a lake. Because the book was made for Spanish readers, the artists may have chosen to explain Mexica history clearly and proudly. Historians always ask who made a source and for whom.")),

dict(area="sociales", n=10, slug="pumpkins", std="SS.6-8.EC.6",
  std_texto="explain how supply and demand affect prices in a market.",
  titulo="The pumpkin price mystery", titulo_es="El misterio del precio de las calabazas",
  entrada="A story about a class pumpkin stand and how supply and demand change prices.",
  entrada_es="Un cuento sobre un puesto de calabazas de la clase y cómo la oferta y la demanda cambian los precios.",
  tema="Supply and demand change prices", tema_es="La oferta y la demanda cambian los precios",
  descripcion="A Grade 6 social studies story: a class pumpkin stand at the fall festival shows how supply and demand change prices. With key words, glossary, questions, an art connection and optional help in Spanish.",
  alt="At an outdoor fall festival, Salma and Athena stand behind a table full of pumpkins while a long line of families waits; Daniel changes the price on a small chalkboard and Ms. Haddad watches with a cup of cider.",
  escena=f"A sunny autumn festival in a Chicago school parking lot with orange and yellow trees: a long wooden table piled with orange pumpkins of many sizes; {SALMA}, and {ATHENA}, stand behind the table smiling; a long line of happy families waits in front; {DANIEL}, erases a small blank chalkboard with a cloth; {P_HADDAD}, stands to the side holding a paper cup of apple cider",
  palabras=[
    ("supply","supply","How much of a product sellers have and are willing to sell.","oferta","Cuánto de un producto tienen los vendedores y están dispuestos a vender."),
    ("demand","demand","How much of a product buyers want and are willing to buy.","demanda","Cuánto de un producto quieren los compradores y están dispuestos a comprar."),
    ("price","price","The amount of money a buyer pays for a product.","precio","La cantidad de dinero que un comprador paga por un producto."),
    ("shortage","shortage","When buyers want more of a product than sellers have.","escasez","Cuando los compradores quieren más de un producto del que tienen los vendedores."),
    ("surplus","surplus","When sellers have more of a product than buyers want.","excedente","Cuando los vendedores tienen más de un producto del que quieren los compradores."),
    ("market","market","Any place where buyers and sellers meet to trade.","mercado","Cualquier lugar donde compradores y vendedores se encuentran para comerciar.")],
  parrafos=[
    '''Every October, the sixth grade ran a pumpkin stand at the school's fall festival. This year, a farm sold them 100 pumpkins for two dollars each. "We'll sell them for five dollars," said Daniel. "Easy profit." Ms. Haddad smiled. "Let's see what the [[market|market]] says."''',
    '''On the first Saturday, two weeks before Halloween, a line of families stretched across the parking lot. By noon, they had sold 60 pumpkins. {{a|"Everybody wants a pumpkin right before Halloween," said Salma. "That's [[demand|demand]]," said Ms. Haddad. "How much people want something and are willing to pay for it."}}''',
    '''But there were only 40 pumpkins left and a long line of people. "We're going to run out," said Athena. {{b|"When buyers want more than sellers have, there's a [[shortage|shortage]]," said Ms. Haddad. "What do you think happens to the [[price|price]]?"}} Daniel erased the chalkboard. Seven dollars. People still bought them, and the last pumpkin was gone by two o'clock.''',
    '''"Are we being unfair?" asked Salma. "That's a good question to ask," said Ms. Haddad. "When there's a shortage, sellers can often raise the price, and some buyers decide to wait or to buy somewhere else. Higher prices also tell farmers that people want more pumpkins." So the class bought 100 more pumpkins for the next Saturday.''',
    '''{{c|"Now our [[supply|supply]] is bigger," said Athena. "We have more pumpkins to sell."}} But on the next Saturday, a farm stand opened across the street, selling pumpkins too. Families had more choices, and the line at the school stand was short. Daniel kept the price at seven dollars. By noon, they had sold only twelve.''',
    '''"Why isn't anyone buying?" said Daniel. {{d|"Now you have more pumpkins than buyers want at that price," said Ms. Haddad. "That's a [[surplus|surplus]]. When there's a surplus, sellers usually have to lower the price."}} Daniel sighed and wrote four dollars. By the end of the day, they had sold 70 in all.''',
    '''On the day after Halloween, they still had 30 pumpkins. Daniel wrote one dollar on the chalkboard, and still nobody came. {{e|"After Halloween, the demand for pumpkins drops a lot," said Ms. Haddad. "People don't need them anymore."}} "So the same pumpkin is worth seven dollars one week and almost nothing the next?" said Daniel. "Exactly. The price depends on supply and demand."''',
    '''In the end, the class sold the last pumpkins to the school cafeteria for pumpkin muffins, and they still made a profit. Daniel added up the money. "Fine," he said. "The market wins." Athena handed him a muffin. "And the muffins win too."'''],
  preguntas=[
    ("a","What does demand mean in the story?","¿Qué significa demanda en el cuento?",
     ["How much people want something and will pay","How many pumpkins a farm can grow in a year","How much money the class needs for a trip"],0,
     "Demand is how much people want something and are willing to pay for it."),
    ("b","What is a shortage?","¿Qué es una escasez?",
     ["When sellers have more than buyers want","When buyers want more than sellers have","When the price is the same every week"],1,
     "When buyers want more than sellers have, there is a shortage."),
    ("c","Why did the class's supply get bigger?","¿Por qué creció la oferta de la clase?",
     ["More families came to the festival","The farm gave them pumpkins for free","They bought 100 more pumpkins to sell"],2,
     "The class bought 100 more pumpkins, so they had more pumpkins to sell."),
    ("d","What usually happens to the price when there's a surplus?","¿Qué suele pasar con el precio cuando hay un excedente?",
     ["It always stays the same","Sellers can raise it a lot","Sellers usually have to lower it"],2,
     "When there is a surplus, sellers usually have to lower the price."),
    ("e","Why did no one buy pumpkins after Halloween?","¿Por qué nadie compró calabazas después de Halloween?",
     ["The pumpkins had all turned rotten","The demand for pumpkins dropped a lot","The price was much too high for families"],1,
     "After Halloween, the demand for pumpkins drops a lot, because people don't need them anymore.")],
  reto=dict(tipo="Predict the price", tipo_es="Predice el precio",
    pregunta="Predict what would happen to the price of pumpkins in each case, and explain using supply or demand: (1) a storm destroys many pumpkin farms; (2) a famous chef says pumpkin soup is the best food in the world.",
    pregunta_es="Predice qué pasaría con el precio de las calabazas en cada caso y explica usando la oferta o la demanda: (1) una tormenta destruye muchas granjas de calabazas; (2) un chef famoso dice que la sopa de calabaza es la mejor comida del mundo.",
    pista="(1) The supply goes down, because there are fewer pumpkins to sell. With fewer pumpkins and the same number of buyers, there is a shortage, so the price goes up. (2) The demand goes up, because more people want pumpkins. If the supply stays the same, the price goes up too."),
  mdc=dict(pregunta="Salma asked if raising the price was unfair. Sometimes, after a disaster, sellers raise the price of water or gas very high. Should there be limits on prices at times like that? Give one reason for and one reason against.",
    pregunta_es="Salma preguntó si subir el precio era injusto. A veces, después de un desastre, los vendedores suben muchísimo el precio del agua o de la gasolina. ¿Debería haber límites a los precios en esos momentos? Da una razón a favor y una en contra.",
    pista="For limits: people need water and gas to stay safe, and very high prices can hurt families who can't pay. Many states, including Illinois, have rules against price gouging during emergencies. Against limits: high prices can push sellers to bring more supplies from far away and help people buy only what they need. Economists disagree, so a good answer explains both sides and then takes a position."),
  pausa=dict(emoji="🖼️", arte="Visual arts · VA:Cn11.1.6",
    intro="Look at The Harvesters, painted by Pieter Bruegel the Elder in 1565, in what is now Belgium. It shows a wheat harvest at the end of summer.",
    intro_es="Observa Los segadores, pintado por Pieter Bruegel el Viejo en 1565, en lo que hoy es Bélgica. Muestra una cosecha de trigo al final del verano.",
    enlace=("https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/435809","See the painting (The Metropolitan Museum of Art)"),
    describe="A golden field of wheat fills most of the painting. Some workers cut the wheat with long blades, others tie it into bundles and carry it away. Under a tree, a group rests and eats lunch while one man sleeps. Far away, you can see a village, a church and ships on the water.",
    describe_es="Un campo dorado de trigo llena casi toda la pintura. Algunos trabajadores cortan el trigo con guadañas y otros lo atan en manojos y se lo llevan. Bajo un árbol, un grupo descansa y come mientras un hombre duerme. A lo lejos se ven un pueblo, una iglesia y barcos en el agua.",
    preguntas=[("How many different jobs can you find in the painting? Where do you think the wheat goes after the harvest?","¿Cuántos trabajos distintos encuentras en la pintura? ¿A dónde crees que va el trigo después de la cosecha?"),
               ("If the harvest is very big one year, what would happen to the price of bread? What if a storm ruined the harvest?","Si un año la cosecha es muy grande, ¿qué pasaría con el precio del pan? ¿Y si una tormenta arruinara la cosecha?")],
    pista="You can find people cutting, tying, carrying, resting and eating, and even someone walking to the village. The wheat would be turned into flour and bread, and some could travel by ship to be sold far away. A big harvest means a bigger supply of wheat, so the price of bread would probably go down. A ruined harvest means a smaller supply and a shortage, so the price would go up.")),

dict(area="sociales", n=11, slug="citizens", std="SS.6-8.CV.1.LC",
  std_texto="describe the roles, rights and responsibilities of citizens in the United States, and explain the different ways a person can become a citizen.",
  titulo="The oath", titulo_es="El juramento",
  entrada="A story about a ceremony where new citizens take an oath, and the rights and responsibilities of citizens.",
  entrada_es="Un cuento sobre una ceremonia en la que nuevos ciudadanos hacen un juramento, y los derechos y responsabilidades de los ciudadanos.",
  tema="Citizens have rights and responsibilities", tema_es="Los ciudadanos tienen derechos y responsabilidades",
  descripcion="A Grade 6 social studies story: students hear about a naturalization ceremony and learn the ways to become a United States citizen and the rights and responsibilities of citizens. With key words, glossary, questions, an art connection and optional help in Spanish.",
  alt="In the school library, Ms. Haddad shows a photo of a smiling older man holding a small American flag at a ceremony, while Sophia, Daniel and Athena make a chart of rights and responsibilities.",
  escena=f"A sixth grade classroom in Chicago: {P_HADDAD}, holds up a large photo of a smiling older Latino man with gray hair, in a suit, holding a small American flag in a courtroom full of happy people; {SOPHIA}, {DANIEL}, and {ATHENA}, sit together drawing a big chart on poster paper with simple drawings of a ballot box, a scale of justice and a speech bubble, with no words",
  palabras=[
    ("citizen","citizen","A member of a country who has the full rights and responsibilities of that country.","ciudadano o ciudadana","Miembro de un país que tiene todos los derechos y responsabilidades de ese país."),
    ("naturalization","naturalization","The legal process by which a person born in another country becomes a citizen.","naturalización","El proceso legal por el que una persona nacida en otro país se hace ciudadana."),
    ("oath","oath","A serious, public promise.","juramento","Una promesa seria y pública."),
    ("right","right","A freedom or protection that the law guarantees to people.","derecho","Una libertad o protección que la ley garantiza a las personas."),
    ("responsibility","responsibility","A duty, something a person is expected to do for their community or country.","responsabilidad","Un deber, algo que se espera que una persona haga por su comunidad o su país."),
    ("jury","jury","A group of citizens who listen to a trial and decide the result.","jurado","Un grupo de ciudadanos que escuchan un juicio y deciden el resultado.")],
  parrafos=[
    '''On Monday, Ms. Haddad showed the class a photo of an older man in a suit, holding a small flag and smiling so widely that his eyes were almost closed. "This is my neighbor, Mr. Herrera," she said. "Last Friday, at the federal courthouse downtown, he became a [[citizen|citizen]] of the United States, together with 90 people from 40 countries."''',
    '''"Wait, he wasn't a citizen before?" asked Daniel. "He was born in another country, and he moved to Chicago many years ago," said Ms. Haddad. {{a|"There are two main ways to become a United States citizen. Most people are citizens because they were born here. Others become citizens later, through a legal process called [[naturalization|naturalization]]."}}''',
    '''"How does naturalization work?" asked Sophia. {{b|"First, a person must live in the country legally for several years," said Ms. Haddad. "Then they fill out an application. They show they can read, write and speak basic English, and they take a test about United States history and government."}} "Like a social studies test?" said Daniel. "Exactly like that. Mr. Herrera studied for months. He even quizzed me."''',
    '''"The last step is the ceremony," she went on. {{c|"The new citizens stand, raise their right hands and take an [[oath|oath]]: a serious promise to support the Constitution and the laws of the United States."}} Athena asked what Mr. Herrera did right after. Ms. Haddad laughed. "He called his daughter, and then he registered to vote, right there in the hallway."''',
    '''"So what changes when you're a citizen?" asked Athena. Ms. Haddad drew a chart with two columns. {{d|"Citizens have special [[right|rights]], like voting in federal elections, running for many offices, like Congress, and getting a United States passport."}} Then she drew a line. "But many rights in the Constitution protect everyone who lives here, citizen or not: freedom of speech, freedom of religion and the right to a fair trial."''',
    '''"What's the other column?" asked Sophia. "[[responsibility|Responsibilities]]," said Ms. Haddad. {{e|"Citizens are expected to vote and stay informed, and when they are called, they serve on a [[jury|jury]], a group of citizens who decide the result of a trial."}} She added more: obey the laws, pay taxes and respect the rights of others. "Those are responsibilities for everyone who lives here."''',
    '''Daniel looked at the chart. "So none of us can vote yet." "Not until you're 18," said Ms. Haddad. "But everyone in this room can already do a lot of what citizens do: learn about issues, speak up, help your community and respect other people's rights. Being a good citizen is a habit you start practicing now."''',
    '''At the end of class, the students wrote cards for Mr. Herrera. Athena wrote: Congratulations! Thank you for studying so hard. Daniel wrote: Welcome to the club. Please tell me the answers to the test, so I'm ready.'''],
  preguntas=[
    ("a","What are the two main ways to become a United States citizen?","¿Cuáles son las dos formas principales de ser ciudadano de Estados Unidos?",
     ["Being born here or naturalization","Voting or serving on a jury","Paying taxes or getting a job"],0,
     "Most people are citizens because they were born in the United States; others become citizens later through naturalization."),
    ("b","What is one step of naturalization?","¿Cuál es un paso de la naturalización?",
     ["Winning an election for Congress","Taking a test on history and government","Living in the country for one week"],1,
     "People who apply take a test about United States history and government and show they know basic English."),
    ("c","What do new citizens promise in the oath?","¿Qué prometen los nuevos ciudadanos en el juramento?",
     ["To move to Washington, D.C.","To vote in every single election","To support the Constitution and the laws"],2,
     "In the oath, new citizens make a serious promise to support the Constitution and the laws of the United States."),
    ("d","Which is a right that only citizens have?","¿Cuál es un derecho que solo tienen los ciudadanos?",
     ["The freedom to practice a religion","Voting in federal elections","A fair trial in court"],1,
     "Voting in federal elections is a special right of citizens; freedom of religion and a fair trial protect everyone."),
    ("e","What is a jury?","¿Qué es un jurado?",
     ["A test that new citizens must pass","The judge who leads a trial in court","A group of citizens who decide a trial"],2,
     "A jury is a group of citizens who listen to a trial and decide the result.")],
  reto=dict(tipo="Rights and responsibilities", tipo_es="Derechos y responsabilidades",
    pregunta="Make a two-column chart: rights and responsibilities. Write two rights and two responsibilities from the story. Then explain why a right, like voting, comes with a responsibility.",
    pregunta_es="Haz una tabla de dos columnas: derechos y responsabilidades. Escribe dos derechos y dos responsabilidades del cuento. Después explica por qué un derecho, como votar, viene con una responsabilidad.",
    pista="Rights: voting in federal elections, freedom of speech, a fair trial. Responsibilities: voting and staying informed, serving on a jury, obeying the laws, paying taxes. Voting is a right, but to use it well, citizens have the responsibility to learn about the candidates and the issues. A democracy works only when people use their rights carefully."),
  mdc=dict(pregunta="Ms. Haddad said Daniel can practice being a good citizen before he can vote. Plan one action a sixth grader could take to help their community. Which right would you be using, and which responsibility would you be meeting?",
    pregunta_es="La maestra Haddad dijo que Daniel puede practicar ser buen ciudadano antes de poder votar. Planea una acción que un estudiante de sexto podría hacer para ayudar a su comunidad. ¿Qué derecho usarías y qué responsabilidad cumplirías?",
    pista="Example: writing a letter to the alderperson asking for a crosswalk near school. You would be using the right to freedom of speech and the right to ask the government for change, and meeting the responsibility to stay informed and take part in your community. Other ideas: organizing a park cleanup or a food drive."),
  pausa=dict(emoji="🗽", arte="Visual arts · VA:Cn11.1.6",
    intro="Look at the Statue of Liberty, in New York Harbor. It was a gift from the people of France and was finished in 1886. Read the famous lines from a poem by Emma Lazarus, written in 1883, which are on a bronze plaque inside the statue's pedestal.",
    intro_es="Observa la Estatua de la Libertad, en el puerto de Nueva York. Fue un regalo del pueblo de Francia y se terminó en 1886. Lee los famosos versos de un poema de Emma Lazarus, escrito en 1883, que están en una placa de bronce dentro del pedestal de la estatua.",
    enlace=("https://www.nps.gov/stli/learn/historyculture/emma-lazarus.htm","Learn about Emma Lazarus and the statue (National Park Service)"),
    versos=["Give me your tired, your poor,","Your huddled masses yearning to breathe free,"],
    versos_fuente="Emma Lazarus, “The New Colossus” (1883)",
    describe="A tall woman in a long robe holds a torch high in her right hand and a tablet in her left. A crown with seven points sits on her head. At her feet is a broken chain.",
    describe_es="Una mujer alta con una túnica larga sostiene una antorcha en alto con la mano derecha y una tablilla en la izquierda. En la cabeza lleva una corona de siete puntas. A sus pies hay una cadena rota.",
    preguntas=[("What do you think the torch and the broken chain stand for?","¿Qué crees que significan la antorcha y la cadena rota?"),
               ("Emma Lazarus imagined the statue speaking to people arriving by ship. How do her words change the way people see the statue?","Emma Lazarus imaginó que la estatua les hablaba a las personas que llegaban en barco. ¿Cómo cambian sus palabras la forma en que la gente ve la estatua?")],
    pista="The torch can stand for light and freedom showing the way, and the broken chain for freedom from being controlled. The tablet has the date of the Declaration of Independence. The sculptor made it as a symbol of liberty, but Emma Lazarus's poem turned it into a symbol of welcome for people coming to start a new life. Art and words together can give a monument a new meaning.")),

dict(area="sociales", n=12, slug="review", std="SS.6-8.H · SS.6-8.G · SS.6-8.CV · SS.6-8.EC",
  std_texto="review of the Grade 6 social studies topics: historical sources, change and continuity, ancient civilizations and their environments, democracy and written laws, trade routes and innovation, supply and demand, and citizenship.",
  titulo="The museum of us", titulo_es="El museo de nosotros",
  entrada="A story about a class museum where each student explains one big idea from the year.",
  entrada_es="Un cuento sobre un museo de la clase en el que cada estudiante explica una gran idea del año.",
  tema="Big ideas of the year connect the past and the present", tema_es="Las grandes ideas del año conectan el pasado y el presente",
  descripcion="A Grade 6 social studies story: at a class museum night, each student explains one big idea of the year and connects it to today. With key words, glossary, questions and optional help in Spanish.",
  alt="At a museum night in the school library, the seven friends stand next to their exhibits: a time capsule, a river model, pottery pieces, a silk scarf, a jar of salt, a lake city model and a chart of rights, while families walk between the tables.",
  escena=f"A school library in Chicago set up as a small museum at night, with warm lights and tables: {DANIEL}, stands by a dented metal box; {SOPHIA}, holds a red silk scarf; {ATHENA}, shows a few pieces of broken reddish pottery; {ELIAS}, holds a jar of white salt; {ISRAEL}, and {SELENA}, stand beside a clay model of an island city on a tray of water; {SALMA}, points at a model of two rivers with tiny canals; {P_HADDAD}, smiles at the entrance; happy families walk between the tables",
  palabras=[
    ("history","history","The study of the past, based on evidence.","historia","El estudio del pasado, basado en evidencia."),
    ("geography","geography","The study of places, the environment and how people interact with them.","geografía","El estudio de los lugares, el medio ambiente y cómo la gente se relaciona con ellos."),
    ("government","government","The people and rules that run a community, a city or a country.","gobierno","Las personas y las reglas que dirigen una comunidad, una ciudad o un país."),
    ("economics","economics","The study of how people make, buy, sell and use goods and services.","economía","El estudio de cómo la gente produce, compra, vende y usa productos y servicios."),
    ("exhibit","exhibit","A display of objects and information that people can visit and learn from.","exhibición","Una muestra de objetos e información que la gente puede visitar para aprender."),
    ("connection","connection","A way two ideas, events or places are related.","conexión","Una forma en que dos ideas, sucesos o lugares están relacionados.")],
  parrafos=[
    '''On the last Thursday of May, the school library turned into a museum. Each student had built an [[exhibit|exhibit]] about one big idea from sixth grade social studies. {{a|"Your job is to teach our visitors," said Ms. Haddad, "and to make a [[connection|connection]] between the past and today."}}''',
    '''Daniel stood by the old metal box from the time capsule. "This is [[history|history]]," he told a group of parents. {{b|"A letter from 1976 is a primary source, because the writer was there. A history book written later is a secondary source. Historians compare many sources, because each person sees things from a different perspective."}}''',
    '''Salma had built two clay rivers with tiny canals. "This is [[geography|geography]]," she explained. {{c|"In Mesopotamia, people settled near rivers because there was very little rain. They dug irrigation canals, grew a surplus of food and built some of the first cities. But the irrigation left salt in the soil."}} A little girl touched the canals. "Like a water slide for plants," she said.''',
    '''Athena held up her pottery pieces. "This is [[government|government]]. In Athens, citizens voted on laws themselves, and they could even vote to send away someone too powerful. But only some men could vote." {{d|"Today, in the United States, all adult citizens can vote, and we elect representatives. And like the Romans with their Twelve Tables, we write our laws down, so everyone can know their rights."}}''',
    '''Sophia waved her silk scarf, and Elías shook his jar of salt. "This is trade," said Sophia. "Silk, paper and ideas traveled along the Silk Road." "And in Mali, gold was traded for salt across the Sahara," said Elías. "Each place traded what it had for what it lacked." A grandfather laughed. "I'd still choose the gold." "Everyone says that," said Elías.''',
    '''Next to them, a sign said [[economics|Economics]], with a picture of pumpkins and prices. {{e|"When demand goes up and supply stays the same, prices go up," Elías explained to a visitor. "After Halloween, demand drops, and pumpkins are almost free."}} The visitor nodded. "That explains my cousin's pumpkin farm."''',
    '''Israel and Selena had their lake city floating on a tray. "The Mexica adapted to a lake and built chinampas," said Selena. "And today, Mexico City is sinking," said Israel, "because water is pumped from under the old lake. Be careful: two things happening together isn't always a cause." Next to them, a chart of rights and responsibilities hung on the wall, with a card for Mr. Herrera that said Welcome to the club.''',
    '''At the end of the night, Ms. Haddad gathered the class. "So what did you learn this year?" Daniel thought for a moment. "That the past isn't over," he said. "We still use Roman ideas, Chinese inventions and Greek words. And somebody, someday, will open our time capsule and study us." Athena smiled. "Then we'd better be interesting."'''],
  preguntas=[
    ("a","What was the students' job at the museum?","¿Cuál era el trabajo de los estudiantes en el museo?",
     ["To sell tickets and snacks to visitors","To teach visitors and connect past and present","To guard the exhibits during the night"],1,
     "Ms. Haddad said their job was to teach the visitors and make a connection between the past and today."),
    ("b","Why do historians compare many sources, according to Daniel?","Según Daniel, ¿por qué los historiadores comparan muchas fuentes?",
     ["Each person sees things differently","Most old sources are lost or broken","Primary sources are always wrong"],0,
     "Historians compare many sources because each person sees things from a different perspective."),
    ("c","What did Salma's exhibit explain about Mesopotamia?","¿Qué explicaba la exhibición de Salma sobre Mesopotamia?",
     ["People left the rivers to live in the desert","People traded silk for gold and salt","People used rivers to grow food and build cities"],2,
     "People settled near the rivers, dug irrigation canals, grew a surplus of food and built some of the first cities."),
    ("d","What ideas did Athens and Rome give to the United States?","¿Qué ideas le dieron Atenas y Roma a Estados Unidos?",
     ["Kings should choose all the laws","Citizens vote, and laws are written down","Only some men should be able to vote"],1,
     "From Athens came citizens voting, and from Rome came writing laws down so everyone can know their rights."),
    ("e","What happens to prices when demand goes up and supply stays the same?","¿Qué pasa con los precios cuando la demanda sube y la oferta sigue igual?",
     ["Prices disappear","Prices go down","Prices go up"],2,
     "When demand goes up and supply stays the same, prices go up.")],
  reto=dict(tipo="Make a connection", tipo_es="Haz una conexión",
    pregunta="Choose two exhibits from the museum and explain a connection between them. For example, how are the Silk Road and supply and demand connected, or Mesopotamia and Tenochtitlan? Use evidence from two stories.",
    pregunta_es="Elige dos exhibiciones del museo y explica una conexión entre ellas. Por ejemplo, ¿cómo se conectan la Ruta de la Seda y la oferta y la demanda, o Mesopotamia y Tenochtitlan? Usa evidencia de dos cuentos.",
    pista="Example: Mesopotamia and Tenochtitlan both show people adapting to water. In Mesopotamia, farmers dug canals to bring water to dry land, and in Tenochtitlan, the Mexica built chinampas in a lake. In both places, these changes helped grow food and cities, but they also caused problems later: salty soil and a sinking city. Another example: silk was scarce in the West, so its price went up at each city on the Silk Road, which is supply and demand."),
  mdc=dict(pregunta="Daniel said that someday someone will study us. Imagine a historian in 2125 studying your sixth grade year. Which three sources would help them most, and what might they misunderstand about your life?",
    pregunta_es="Daniel dijo que algún día alguien nos estudiará. Imagina a un historiador en 2125 que estudia tu año de sexto grado. ¿Qué tres fuentes le ayudarían más y qué podría entender mal de tu vida?",
    pista="Helpful sources could be a class photo, a student's journal and a school newsletter. A historian might misunderstand things if they saw only one source: a photo of a party could make it seem like every day was a party, and a news story about one problem could make it seem like the whole year was bad. That's why they would need many sources and many perspectives, just like we did."),
  pausa=dict(emoji="🏛️", arte="Visual arts · VA:Cn11.1.6",
    intro="Look back at the art you met this year: the Standard of Ur, the blue hippo from Egypt, the coin from Athens, the Pont du Gard, the Tang camel, the Diamond Sutra, the Catalan Atlas, the Codex Mendoza, The Harvesters and the Statue of Liberty.",
    intro_es="Repasa el arte que conociste este año: el Estandarte de Ur, el hipopótamo azul de Egipto, la moneda de Atenas, el Pont du Gard, el camello Tang, el Sutra del Diamante, el Atlas Catalán, el Códice Mendoza, Los segadores y la Estatua de la Libertad.",
    preguntas=[("Which work of art taught you the most about the people who made it? What did it show about their time, their beliefs or their resources?","¿Qué obra de arte te enseñó más sobre las personas que la hicieron? ¿Qué mostraba sobre su época, sus creencias o sus recursos?"),
               ("If an artist made one work today to show our time to people in the future, what should it show? Explain why.","Si un artista hiciera hoy una obra para mostrar nuestra época a la gente del futuro, ¿qué debería mostrar? Explica por qué.")],
    pista="Any choice works if you connect the art to its time. For example, the Standard of Ur shows a surplus of food and trade with faraway places, the Catalan Atlas shows how far news about Mali traveled, and The Harvesters shows the work of a whole village at harvest time. A work about today might show phones and screens, city neighborhoods, families from many places or people caring for the environment. Art reflects the times, traditions and resources of the people who make it.")),
]
